  "content_blocks": [
    {
      "block_id": "b00000",
      "block_type": "heading",
      "text": "What is procurement oversight?",
      "ordinal": 0,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00001",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "1. Procurement oversight is intended to help ensure the requirements of the Procurement Act 2023 (Act) are adhered to, through the provision of recommendations and guidance, following investigation of a contracting authority’s compliance. This will help to ensure the benefits of the legislation are achieved.",
      "ordinal": 1,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00002",
      "block_type": "heading",
      "text": "What is the legal framework that governs procurement oversight?",
      "ordinal": 2,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "What is the legal framework that governs procurement oversight?"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00003",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "2. Part 10 of the Act (Procurement Oversight) comprises three provisions which enable the procurement oversight regime to support contracting authorities’ compliance with the requirements of the Act:",
      "ordinal": 3,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "What is the legal framework that governs procurement oversight?"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00004",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "a. Section 108 (Procurement investigations) provides that an appropriate authority may investigate a relevant contracting authority’s compliance with requirements of the Act and require a relevant contracting authority to provide documents and give assistance in connection with the investigation, as is reasonable;",
      "ordinal": 4,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "What is the legal framework that governs procurement oversight?"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00005",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "b. Section 109 (Recommendations following procurement investigations) provides that, following a procurement investigation, an appropriate authority may issue a recommendation (a section 109 recommendation) to a relevant contracting authority, if it considers that the contracting authority is engaging in action which is causing, or that is likely to cause, a breach of any requirement of the Act. The contracting authority must have regard to the section 109 recommendation when considering how to comply with the requirements of the Act;",
      "ordinal": 5,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "What is the legal framework that governs procurement oversight?"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00006",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "c. Section 110 (Guidance following procurement investigations) provides that, following a procurement investigation, an appropriate authority may publish ‘lessons learned’ guidance, to support compliance with the requirements of the Act by contracting authorities generally. Contracting authorities must have regard to this guidance when considering how to comply with the requirements of the Act.",
      "ordinal": 6,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "What is the legal framework that governs procurement oversight?"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00007",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "3. The oversight powers in Part 10 may be exercised by an appropriate authority (which is defined in section 123 of the Act as ‘a Minister of the Crown, the Welsh Ministers, or a Northern Ireland department’) within the limits set by the Act (see sections 111, 112 and 113).",
      "ordinal": 7,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "What is the legal framework that governs procurement oversight?"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00008",
      "block_type": "heading",
      "text": "What has changed?",
      "ordinal": 8,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "What has changed?"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00009",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "4. The Small Business, Enterprise and Employment Act 2015 (SBEEA) gave powers to the Minister for the Cabinet Office or Secretary of State to investigate a contracting authority’s exercise of relevant functions relating to procurement. The Act repeals these powers and replaces them with the powers in Part 10 , which explicitly relate to compliance with the Act. To strengthen the ability to tackle any non-compliance, the Act introduces new powers to issue recommendations to a contracting authority following an investigation to improve compliance (and to require progress reports on the implementation of recommendations) and to issue lessons learned guidance to all contracting authorities following an investigation.",
      "ordinal": 9,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "What has changed?"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00010",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "5. ‘Maintained schools’ (as defined in the Education Act 2002 ) and ‘academies’ (as defined in the Education Act 1996 and Academies Act 2010 ) were not within scope of investigation under the SBEEA but are in scope of the new oversight regime in the Act.",
      "ordinal": 10,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "What has changed?"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00011",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "6. The Public Procurement Review Service (PPRS), which sits within the Cabinet Office, historically carried out investigations into procurement functions under the SBEEA on behalf of the Minister. These investigations were initiated by complaints from suppliers about a particular procurement or a late payment issue.The PPRS continues to operate on this basis, but utilising the powers provided by section 108 of the Act and on a non-statutory basis where the complaint falls outside the scope of the Act (i.e. the procurement is governed by the Public Contracts Regulations 2015). The PPRS carries out those investigations into compliance with the Act, together with the new Procurement Compliance Service (PCS), both services sit in the Procurement Compliance and Oversight (PCO), which has been established to operate the procurement oversight regime.",
      "ordinal": 11,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "What has changed?"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00012",
      "block_type": "heading",
      "text": "Key points and policy intent",
      "ordinal": 12,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00013",
      "block_type": "heading",
      "text": "The role of PCO",
      "ordinal": 13,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "The role of PCO"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00014",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "7. PCO will manage the oversight regime on behalf of a Minister of the Crown.",
      "ordinal": 14,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "The role of PCO"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00015",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "8. PCS may monitor compliance across contracting authorities and will focus on investigations into contracting authorities which demonstrate patterns of repeated non-compliance across its procurements (referred to as institutional non-compliance). A pattern of non-compliance could also be across a number of different contracting authorities (referred to as systemic non-compliance) and this could mean that a number of contracting authorities are investigated in parallel.",
      "ordinal": 15,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "The role of PCO"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00016",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "9. The Minister will have oversight of any section 109 recommendation issued to a contracting authority following a procurement investigation. PCO will manage the publication of any investigation reports, including any recommendations issued, and progress reports submitted by contracting authorities (or notice of a contracting authority’s failure to submit a progress report). Recommendations will be published on a dedicated gov.uk page, which will also serve as the mechanism for suppliers and other stakeholders to raise a concern regarding a contracting authority’s compliance with PCO.",
      "ordinal": 16,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "The role of PCO"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00017",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "10. PCO will also work with other central commercial teams, as appropriate, to manage the release of any guidance resulting from a procurement investigation.",
      "ordinal": 17,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "The role of PCO"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00018",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "11. The oversight regime applies to almost all contracting authorities that are subject to the Act (see section 108(5) for the definition of ‘relevant contracting authority’), with the exception of private utilities (as independent regulators already provide oversight of these utilities). Welsh contracting authorities should refer to the Welsh-specific guidance for information on how the oversight regime will be managed on behalf of Welsh Ministers.",
      "ordinal": 18,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "The role of PCO"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00019",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "12. Government departments are excluded from the scope of the investigatory powers in the Act since Ministers already have non-statutory powers to oversee their contracting activities.",
      "ordinal": 19,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "The role of PCO"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00020",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "13. An appropriate authority is able to issue guidance under section 110 to all contracting authorities, whether subject to the section 108 investigatory powers or not.",
      "ordinal": 20,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "The role of PCO"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00021",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "14. The threshold for launching a statutory investigation is high, only matters which constitute a severe breach of compliance at a systemic or institutional level will result in a PCS investigation.",
      "ordinal": 21,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "The role of PCO"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00022",
      "block_type": "heading",
      "text": "Procurement investigations",
      "ordinal": 22,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "Procurement investigations"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00023",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "15. An appropriate authority may investigate a relevant contracting authority’s compliance with the Act at any time however, it is most likely that an investigation will be initiated as a result of a complaint or referral to the appropriate authority or where there is other evidence of non-compliance (for example, from analysis of data on the central digital platform).",
      "ordinal": 23,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "Procurement investigations"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00024",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "16. If an investigation is being conducted, an appropriate authority may, by notice, require a contracting authority to provide relevant documentation as is reasonably required for the purposes of the investigation and/or request other assistance as is reasonable in the circumstances. This could include requiring the contracting authority to make personnel available to be interviewed. The notice issued by the appropriate authority will provide details of the documentation or assistance required, including the form of documentation.",
      "ordinal": 24,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "Procurement investigations"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00025",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "17. Initiating notices will likely be sent to a named point of contact on procurement notices and the relevant commercial director or equivalent. Depending on the nature of the investigation, subsequent notices or correspondence may be sent directly to a senior specific individual, for example if it relates to the procedure that that individual is managing.",
      "ordinal": 25,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "Procurement investigations"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00026",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "18. A contracting authority that receives a notice as part of a procurement investigation must comply within the timeframe specified in the notice, which must be at least 30 days beginning with the day the notice is given. A contracting authority may request additional time to comply with the notice, but the appropriate authority is not obliged to agree. However, where a longer time frame is agreed, it will supersede the date given in the original notice.",
      "ordinal": 26,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "Procurement investigations"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00027",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "19. The results of the investigation, including any recommendations issued, may be published.",
      "ordinal": 27,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "Procurement investigations"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00028",
      "block_type": "heading",
      "text": "Recommendations following procurement investigations",
      "ordinal": 28,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "Recommendations following procurement investigations"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00029",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "20. Where an appropriate authority has conducted a procurement investigation and, as a result, considers that the contracting authority is acting in a way that either breaches, or is likely to breach, the requirements of the Act, the appropriate authority may issue a section 109 recommendation to that contracting authority.",
      "ordinal": 29,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "Recommendations following procurement investigations"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00030",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "21. Section 109(4) imposes a legal obligation on a contracting authority to ‘have regard’ to any section 109 recommendation that has been issued to it when carrying out covered procurements. To fulfil the duty to have regard to the section 109 recommendation, a contracting authority is not obliged to follow the recommendation, but it must properly consider and engage with it and, if a contracting authority decides not to follow it, it must have clear reasons for doing so and should record these in writing.",
      "ordinal": 30,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "Recommendations following procurement investigations"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00031",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "22. Section 109 recommendations are intended to bring contracting authorities back into compliance with the requirements of the Act and will specify the action(s) that the relevant contracting authority(ies) should take to achieve this, as well as the timing of these steps.",
      "ordinal": 31,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "Recommendations following procurement investigations"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00032",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "23. Section 109(3) sets out some limitations on the scope of section 109 recommendations; these are intended to maintain the contracting authority’s autonomy regarding how it, for example, implements procurement objectives and to protect the probity of the procurement procedure. Section 109 recommendations must not, therefore, relate to how the contracting authority:",
      "ordinal": 32,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "Recommendations following procurement investigations"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00033",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "a. complies with section 12 (procurement objectives) and section 86 (regulated below-threshold contracts: duty to consider SMEs);",
      "ordinal": 33,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "Recommendations following procurement investigations"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00034",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "b. has regard to the national procurement policy statement under section 13 or the Wales procurement policy statement under section 14 (as applicable);",
      "ordinal": 34,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "Recommendations following procurement investigations"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00035",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "c. exercises its discretion in relation to a particular procurement, i.e. a section 109 recommendation must not relate to a decision taken in respect of an individual procurement procedure.",
      "ordinal": 35,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "Recommendations following procurement investigations"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00036",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "24. However, section 109 does not prevent other, non-statutory recommendations being made with a view to addressing non-compliance relating to an individual procurement or to address other areas of procurement practice that fall outside of the boundaries of section 109 recommendations. It is therefore possible for non-statutory recommendations to be made as a result of a complaint raised by a supplier about a specific procurement. And it is possible that an investigation into repeated non-compliance could lead to both statutory and non-statutory recommendations being issued to a contracting authority. The appropriate authority will need to be clear about the status of the recommendations issued.",
      "ordinal": 36,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "Recommendations following procurement investigations"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00037",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "25. Sections 109(5) to 109(8) make provision for a contracting authority to submit progress reports, where this is required by the section 109 recommendation. Where required to do so, a contracting authority must submit a progress report setting out the action it has taken as a result of the recommendation. If no or different action (to that specified in the recommendation) has been taken, the progress report must include the contracting authority’s reasons for this.",
      "ordinal": 37,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "Recommendations following procurement investigations"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00038",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "26. The appropriate authority may publish these progress reports or, if the contracting authority has failed to submit one, a notice of that failure.",
      "ordinal": 38,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "Recommendations following procurement investigations"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00039",
      "block_type": "heading",
      "text": "Guidance following procurement investigations",
      "ordinal": 39,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "Guidance following procurement investigations"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00040",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "27. Section 110 allows an appropriate authority to issue guidance following a procurement investigation, which contracting authorities are required to have regard to (see paragraph 15) when considering how to comply with the requirements of the Act.",
      "ordinal": 40,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "Guidance following procurement investigations"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00041",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "28. This guidance will adopt a ‘lessons learned’ approach and will allow an appropriate authority to tackle compliance concerns identified by a procurement investigation which may affect a broader range of contracting authorities. All contracting authorities are subject to section 110 , but an appropriate authority can target guidance to the types of contracting authorities that would benefit from the particular lessons.",
      "ordinal": 41,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "Guidance following procurement investigations"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00042",
      "block_type": "p",
      "text": "29. A Minister of the Crown may, with express consent from the relevant devolved administrations, be able to issue guidance to devolved Welsh authorities and NI Departments (consent is not technically required for a Minister of the Crown to issue guidance to transferred NI authorities), see section 113(3) and (5).",
      "ordinal": 42,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Key points and policy intent",
        "Guidance following procurement investigations"
      ],
      "source_locator": "details.body",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "block_id": "b00043",
      "block_type": "heading",
      "text": "Where can I go for more information or training",
      "ordinal": 43,
      "heading_path": [
        "What is procurement oversight?",
        "Where can I go for more information or training"
      ],
